<!doctype html><html lang="en-GB"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Workflow tools · Umicom Framework</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../assets/site.css"><script defer src="../assets/site.js"></script>@UMICOM_SITE_ICONS@</head><body><header class="top">@UMICOM_SITE_BRAND@<nav><a href="index.html">Wiki</a><a href="../source-map.html">Source map</a></nav></header><main id="content"><h1>Practical workflow tools across Umicom applications</h1>
<p>A useful application helps you finish a task without losing track of your work. In this lesson you will move between source files in Studio, prepare a fresh order ticket in Trader, find applications in Desk, and review financial amounts in Bank and TMS.</p>
<p>The screens have different purposes, but they share a design rule: the application asks Framework to perform a well-defined operation. Framework owns the calculation or state change. A button is the way you request that operation, not a separate implementation of it.</p>
<nav aria-label="Contents"><a href="#studio">Studio</a><a href="#trader">Trader</a><a href="#desk">Desk</a><a href="#bank">Bank</a><a href="#tms">TMS</a><a href="#contracts">Contracts</a><a href="#example">Complete example</a><a href="#files">Source map</a><a href="#tests">Testing</a></nav>
<h2 id="studio">1. Move between source documents in Studio</h2>
<p>Open a disposable Umicom Notes project with three files: <code>notes.c</code>, <code>notes.h</code> and <code>tests.c</code>. Open them in that order. Add a comment to <code>notes.c</code>, but leave it unsaved.</p>
<p>Choose <strong>Navigate → Next Source Document</strong>, or press <strong>Ctrl+Tab</strong>. Choose <strong>Previous Source Document</strong>, or press <strong>Ctrl+Shift+Tab</strong>, to move the other way. The sequence wraps around: after the last open document comes the first.</p>
<p>The order is the document coordinator’s <em>opening order</em>. It is not a list of recently visited files and does not follow a manually rearranged visual tab order. Only managed source documents participate; a tool panel is not a source document. With one document open, the command keeps that document active. With no active managed source document, the command is unavailable.</p>
<p>Return to <code>notes.c</code>. Your comment, caret, selection and Undo history should still be there. Switching a tab does not save a file, reload it, change its text or discard another draft. Read-only and pinned documents can still be visited.</p>
<h2 id="trader">2. Begin a fresh order ticket</h2>
<p>Use Trader’s simulation environment and select an instrument. Change the ticket’s side, quantity and price. The <strong>Reset ticket</strong> button returns the draft to the existing defaults: Buy, Limit, Day and a quantity of one.</p>
<p>The selected instrument and account remain selected. Framework uses its existing instrument-selection routine to supply the initial limit price: the latest quote midpoint, a retained bar close when no quote exists, or zero when there is no usable price. The stop price returns to zero. Always check the instrument, quantity and price before submitting.</p>
<p>Reset clears the earlier risk-preview result. It does not cancel an order, reverse a fill, reset an emergency stop, change the environment, arm live trading or contact a broker. Existing orders and positions remain in their own stores. Resetting the ticket is therefore different from cancelling an order.</p>
<p>Try it with a simulation order already listed. Record its identifier and status, reset the ticket, and confirm that the order is unchanged. Repeat with the emergency stop engaged: reset must not release that stop.</p>
<h2 id="desk">3. Find applications in Desk</h2>
<p>The category selector on Desk’s Home page works together with its name search. Begin with <strong>All applications</strong>, type part of an application name, and then select a category.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>What it shows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>All applications</td><td>Every application that also matches the name search.</td></tr><tr><td>Available</td><td>Applications whose current launch-choice snapshot reports them eligible.</td></tr><tr><td>Running</td><td>Applications whose snapshot reports them running.</td></tr><tr><td>Selected</td><td>Applications currently selected in the catalogue, whether or not running.</td></tr><tr><td>Needs attention</td><td>Applications with an Attention or Failed runtime state.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Filtering changes visibility only. It does not start or stop a process and does not change the selected applications. A running application can be hidden by a filter and still be running. Choose All applications and clear the name search to show the complete catalogue again.</p>
<h2 id="bank">4. Review account funds in Bank</h2>
<p>Open the <strong>Account funds review — manual values</strong> section above the Bank workstation. Enter <code>GBP</code> and select two decimal places. The currency code identifies the unit; the decimal-place setting tells the calculation how to represent it. The setting is explicit rather than looked up from a currency database.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Amount</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ledger balance</td><td>1000.00</td><td>The starting balance supplied for this exercise.</td></tr><tr><td>Reserved funds</td><td>100.00</td><td>Money already set aside.</td></tr><tr><td>Proposed debit</td><td>250.00</td><td>An amount you are considering, not a payment instruction.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Choose <strong>Calculate</strong>. Available funds should be <strong>900.00</strong>, and the amount remaining after the proposed debit should be <strong>650.00</strong>. Change the debit and notice that the previous result is marked out of date until you calculate again.</p>
<p>This section is a manual calculation. It neither reads your real account balance nor creates a payment, approval or ledger entry. A negative result is shown rather than hidden, but it grants no overdraft permission. Clear amounts resets only these input fields; it does not clear saved financial data.</p>
<h2 id="tms">5. Review a cash forecast in TMS</h2>
<p>Open <strong>Cash forecast review — manual values</strong>. Use GBP, two decimal places, and the values below.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Opening cash</td><td>1000.00</td></tr><tr><td>Inflows</td><td>400.00</td></tr><tr><td>Outflows</td><td>550.00</td></tr><tr><td>Required buffer</td><td>200.00</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The net flow is <code>400.00 − 550.00 = −150.00</code>. Projected cash is <code>1000.00 − 150.00 = 850.00</code>. Buffer headroom is <code>850.00 − 200.00 = 650.00</code>.</p>
<p>Headroom means the amount left above the buffer you supplied. A negative headroom indicates a shortfall relative to that buffer. It is not a lending decision or a complete liquidity-risk measure. The calculation uses one currency and one simple period; it does not discount cashflows, convert currencies, apply calendars or create a trade or settlement.</p>
<h2 id="contracts">6. Use the contracts in another application</h2>
<p>A C <em>header</em> declares what a function accepts and returns. Its <em>implementation</em> performs the operation. A caller checks the returned status before using an output. All new review and filtering calls copy their results to caller-owned storage and retain no input pointers.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Contract</th><th>Purpose and example</th><th>Ownership and failure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>UmiDecimalParse</code></td><td>Convert counted decimal text to a signed integer coefficient at an explicit scale. <code>12.5</code> at scale 2 becomes 1250.</td><td>Borrowed input during the call. Output unchanged on failure; no allocation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDecimalFormat</code></td><td>Turn coefficient 1250, scale 2 back into <code>12.50</code>.</td><td>Caller supplies a buffer including room for its terminating zero. Too little space leaves it unchanged.</td></tr><tr><td><code>umi_decimal_rescale</code></td><td>Change scale with checked multiplication. Reducing scale truncates towards zero.</td><td>Only scales 0–9. No signed overflow is evaluated; rejected output remains unchanged.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiFinanceReviewAccount</code></td><td>Calculate ledger minus reservations, then minus the proposed debit.</td><td>All amounts use the same currency and scale. Reservations and debit must be nonnegative.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiFinanceReviewCash</code></td><td>Calculate net flow, projected cash and headroom above a buffer.</td><td>Nonnegative inflows, outflows and buffer; signed opening balance. Intermediate overflow is rejected.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDocumentCoordinatorCycle</code></td><td>Move to the next or previous managed source document; direction is +1 or −1.</td><td>Borrowed coordinator, owner-thread call. It activates through the existing workbench and does not write a file.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiGtk4AdapterCycleDocument</code></td><td>Use the same source-cycle operation in a GTK host.</td><td>Uses the existing editing binding and completion callback.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiTradingWorkspaceResetDraft</code></td><td>Return the ticket to the existing workspace defaults.</td><td>Workspace-owner call. Clears only draft/risk-preview state; one revision increment.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiTradingUiControllerResetDraft</code></td><td>Use Reset from a menu, button or registered trading action.</td><td>Delegates to the workspace and sends one existing change notification.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDeskApplicationFilterMatches</code></td><td>Ask whether a copied launch choice belongs in a category.</td><td>Pure query; does not change the catalogue or process state.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiGtk4FinanceReviewCreate</code></td><td>Construct the reusable account or cash review widget.</td><td>Returns a floating GTK widget. Adopt it into one parent. It owns its input state and borrows no application service.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiBankRuntimeReviewFunds</code> and <code>UmiTmsRuntimeReviewCash</code></td><td>Thin product entry points for the same Framework review calculations.</td><td>No product-specific arithmetic or extra storage is introduced.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>Why the amount stays an integer</h3>
<p>With two decimal places, <code>1250.75</code> is stored as the integer <code>125075</code> and the scale <code>2</code>. Parsing does not first convert through a floating-point value. Formatting produces exactly the selected number of decimal places.</p>
<p>Use a decimal point, not a comma, and provide no more fractional digits than the selected scale. Optional signs and surrounding spaces or tabs are accepted. Exponent notation, embedded zero bytes and values outside the signed 64-bit range are rejected. The counted input limit is 96 bytes. If rescaling cannot be represented, keep the previous value and ask for a suitable amount rather than wrapping around.</p>
<h3>Connect the widget without copying its logic</h3>
<p>Create the component on the GTK thread, then append it to the host’s existing box:</p>
<pre><code>GtkWidget *review = NULL;
UmiStatus status = UmiGtk4FinanceReviewCreate(
    UMI_GTK4_FINANCE_REVIEW_ACCOUNT, &amp;review);
if (status == UMI_STATUS_OK) {
    gtk_box_append(GTK_BOX(contentBox), review);
}</code></pre>
<p>Here <code>contentBox</code> is an existing GTK box owned by the application window. Appending adopts the component’s floating reference. Bank and TMS use this pattern above their existing workstations, leaving the normal layout and service-backed panels in place.</p>
<p>For a custom screen, call the pure review functions and display the returned fields yourself. Do not assume that a successful calculation authorises a financial action. A real payment or trade still requires its own service, permissions and business checks.</p>
<h2 id="example">7. Compile the complete C example</h2>
<p>The example below uses Framework’s real decimal, money-review and application-filter contracts. It performs the Bank and TMS calculations and checks the expected integer results. The application snapshot used for the final filter exercise is explicitly constructed in memory; it does not inspect or start a real process.</p>
<pre><code>@UMICOM_SITE_WORKFLOW_TOOLS_EXAMPLE@</code></pre>
<p>Save the programme as <code>framework/examples/workflow_tools/main.c</code>, or use the supplied file. In a configured Applications checkout, build and run its existing target:</p>
<pre><code>cmake --build --preset windows-ucrt64-all-debug --target umicom-workflow-tools-example --parallel 2
.\build\windows-ucrt64-all-debug\bin\umicom-workflow-tools-example.exe</code></pre>
<p>On Linux, use the matching native preset and executable:</p>
<pre><code>cmake --build --preset linux-all-gtk4-debug --target umicom-workflow-tools-example --parallel 2
./build/linux-all-gtk4-debug/bin/umicom-workflow-tools-example</code></pre>
<p>The example directory also supports <code>find_package(UmicomFramework CONFIG REQUIRED)</code> when building against an installed SDK. Set <code>CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH</code> to that SDK’s installation directory. The target checks its results with ordinary conditions, so the checks remain active in optimised builds.</p>
<h2 id="files">8. Find the source and its tests</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Location</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>framework/include/umicom/finance/decimal.h</code> and <code>src/finance/decimal.c</code></td><td>Exact text representation and checked rescaling. Start with test_decimal.c for boundaries.</td></tr><tr><td><code>framework/include/umicom/finance_ui/funds_review.h</code> and <code>src/finance_ui/funds_review.c</code></td><td>Shared account/cash review over the existing money arithmetic.</td></tr><tr><td><code>framework/adapters/gtk4/finance_review_gtk4.c</code></td><td>Input fields, validation messages, Calculate and Clear; no payment or trade service.</td></tr><tr><td><code>framework/src/document/coordinator.c</code></td><td>Managed-document cycling through the existing workbench.</td></tr><tr><td><code>applications/studio/src/gui/workbench/runtime/</code></td><td>Native route, availability and document_tabs.inc key profile.</td></tr><tr><td><code>framework/src/trading/workspace.c</code> and <code>src/trading_ui/action_controller.c</code></td><td>Ticket reset, risk-preview invalidation and one controller notification.</td></tr><tr><td><code>framework/adapters/gtk4/trading_ui/trading_panels_gtk4.c</code></td><td>Reset ticket button connected to that controller.</td></tr><tr><td><code>framework/src/desktop/application_filter.c</code> and <code>adapters/gtk4/desk_gtk4.c</code></td><td>Category predicate and Home-page selector; combines with existing search.</td></tr><tr><td><code>applications/bank/src/gtk/workstation.c</code> and <code>applications/tms/src/gtk/workstation.c</code></td><td>Thin composition of the new review above the existing workstation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>framework/tests/workflow_tools/</code></td><td>Decimal, reviews, source tabs, ticket reset, filters and GTK review fixtures.</td></tr><tr><td><code>applications/bank/tests/test_workflow_tools.c</code> and <code>applications/tms/tests/test_workflow_tools.c</code></td><td>Checks of the actual product entry points.</td></tr><tr><td><code>framework/examples/workflow_tools/</code></td><td>This complete example and its source/installed-SDK CMake configuration.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="tests">9. Test the result, not only the click</h2>
<p>After switching a source tab, verify the text and selection you left behind. After resetting a ticket, compare existing order identifiers, quantities and statuses. After filtering Desk, verify that no process was started or stopped and that selection is unchanged. For each financial review, compare the result with the explicit arithmetic above, then change an input and check that the previous result is invalidated.</p>
<p>Use <strong>Report problem</strong> to record a short interaction, add an expected-versus-actual note and export the report. Screenshots are optional and can contain visible private data. Use disposable projects and simulation records, review the exported evidence, and share only what is needed to reproduce the failure.</p>
<p>The review component supplies stable control identifiers such as <code>finance.review.calculate</code> and <code>finance.review.result</code>. An automated GTK fixture uses those IDs to enter values, request Calculate and inspect the result. Keep native keyboard, focus and installed-application checks alongside these toolkit tests.</p>
<h3>Try a further exercise</h3>
<p>Change the proposed debit to <code>950.00</code>. Predict the remaining amount before calculating it. Then change an inflow to a value with three decimal places while the scale remains two. Explain why the first request should produce a negative result, while the second should be rejected without silently rounding.</p>
